Abstract

Smartphone prices vary substantially across models because of differences in hardware specifications and other product characteristics. This project studies the relationship between RAM capacity and smartphone price using a smartphone specifications dataset and supervised learning techniques in Python. The analysis begins with data inspection, missing-value checks, duplicate checks, outlier identification and descriptive statistics. Exploratory analysis is then used to examine RAM distribution, price distribution, the RAM–price relationship, average price by RAM capacity and correlations among numerical features. A RAM-only linear regression model is compared with a Multiple Linear Regression model that uses RAM and ten additional numerical smartphone specifications. The multiple regression model achieved an R² of 0.6724 and an Adjusted R² of 0.6465 on the test set, with MAE of ₹10,069.18 and RMSE of ₹16,856.03. The RAM-only model achieved an R² of 0.2474, indicating that RAM alone explains substantially less of the observed price variation than the combined feature set. The findings show a clear positive statistical association between RAM and price, while also demonstrating that smartphone pricing depends on multiple specifications.

Keywords

Smartphone pricing, RAM, exploratory data analysis, correlation, supervised learning, Multiple Linear Regression, R², Adjusted R², MAE, RMSE

